\f1\fs22 Ominous, too, that grimy yellow light burning in the entryway, hours after sunrise. A dim, narrow hall promised to open out onto a slightly less dim living space. To her left, through an archway, was a tired kitchen: tall stasis closet and cooking stations, cupboards under a bench leading into the lounge. Two high stools jammed against the island; beyond those, a cheap four-seater dining set shoved against floor-to-ceiling curtains, their hems frayed and filthy. Outside, under-Layer grey light seeped through, giving the whole place a sallow smear.\
The whole apartment stank of stale beer, and Hayley soon found the culprit: a case of twenty-four cans crash-landed on the carpet, one edge crushed and leaking, a clean circular puncture dead centre. She picked up the carton; on the far side, a ragged exit wound, the cardboard soaked.\
Particle pistol, she thought automatically. Government agent or crim? Same thing, these days. Nobody had bothered to send in a cleaner.\
The couch sat at a cockeyed angle, one corner jammed up against the curtained window. On its back was a splash of dried blood\'97no sign of more elsewhere, but plenty of mess to tell its own story.\
The bedroom nearly gave Hayley vertigo: a jarring switch from caveman chic and gunfight saloon to minimalist calm. The bed made with Zen focus. Designer-fluffed pillows in strict formation. It screamed feminine attention to comfort, a world apart from the chaos outside.\
She rifled the wardrobe, finding clothing freshly laundered yet tired and faded\'97men\'92s tees, jeans, overalls stitched with 
\fs20 ENCHANTED
\f2\b  
\f1\b0 CLEANING
\fs22 . A drawer of underwear, tossed loose, socks and jocks tumbled like salad greens. Hayley grinned at the half-dozen pairs of battered pink boxers cheerfully printed with cartoon fairies.\
Her eyebrows shot up when she spotted the small Airborne-branded overnight bag. It nestled in a meticulously cleared space on the wardrobe floor, other shoes and boots carefully shoved aside to give it centre stage, like a relic on display. Hayley carried it to the bed and checked inside: barely a change of clothes\'97blouse, pants, knickers\'97well perfumed and tossed, worn. A few strands of blonde hair clung to the lining; she double-bagged them for evidence.\
The bathroom was spotless, reeking of the same expensive perfume as the bag\'97plus vodka, if she nailed her guess. No male spatter around the loo, just an arsenal of Airborne-brand creams and burn ointments, proper bandages, and unopened toothbrushes. A shiny set of nail clippers sat on the vanity; the bin was lightly stuffed with bloody swabs and dressings.\
Whoever got hurt here\'97if Chantara was the nightclub bomber\'97looked a lot more like a bungling amateur than a slick terrorist or methodical serial. Hayley\'92s instincts rebelled at the whole idea.\
But what about Sarah? Was she a kidnap victim, another Scarlet Shower Surgeon notch, or playing a completely different game?\
Anthony Chantara. Sarah Jacobson. Were they a couple? Hayley doubted it. Something about this place felt performative, not lived-in. There were at least two frequenters of this apartment, though she knew better than to assume \'93man and woman.\'94 Could Chantara have kept Jacobson prisoner? The bedroom and bathroom were too well-tended for that; prisoners rarely left behind a scented, polished flat. And if she was captive, where was Chantara sleeping? No spare pillows or blankets in sight. More likely, Jacobson had chosen to stay, coming and going as she pleased. Perhaps now, with those plush medical supplies in the bathroom, she was here nursing Chantara after the bombing at the Stickett Inn.\
In the garage, Hayley found an empty bay with a cage at the back, cleaning supplies inside, matched to Chantara\'92s work logo. Two arcs of fresh tyre rubber snaked across the concrete, starting at the empty bay and drifting a few metres toward the carpark exit, as if someone\'92d left in a hurry.
